    "placesVisited": ["Haridwar", "Gaurikund", "Kedarnath Temple", "Mandakini River", "Kedarnath Valley", "Himalayan Glacier"],
    "story": "The train to Haridwar was packed with pilgrims, all bound for the same sacred hills. We arrived at dawn and the station was already alive — vendors, sadhus, families with children, everyone moving in the same direction.\n\nThe drive to Gaurikund the next day wound through cedar forests and narrow gorges, the road hugging the Mandakini all the way up. The base camp at Gaurikund smelled of wood smoke and chai, and we slept early, knowing the real climb started at sunrise.\n\nThe 16-km trek to Kedarnath took everything we had. The trail switches between stone steps, mountain streams, and sheer rocky faces. We overtook mule trains and were overtaken by older pilgrims who seemed to float upward. By the time the temple came into view — that ancient stone tower rising against the white peaks — the exhaustion vanished.\n\nThe two days at Kedarnath were unlike anything else. The air is so thin and clear that the mountains feel close enough to touch. We woke before dawn on the last morning to walk the glacier path, and watched the sunrise set the peaks on fire one by one.\n\nComing down was its own kind of pilgrimage."
